Description: Implementation of the High-dimensional detection of Landscape
    Dynamics 2 (HILANDYN2) framework (Morresi et al., submitted) for mapping
    stand-replacing and non-stand-replacing forest disturbance using 
    intra-annual optical time series. High-dimensional optical time series 
    include information from the spatial and spectral dimensions and are 
    analysed using the High-dimensional Trend Segmentation (HiTS) procedure 
    proposed by Maeng (2019). The HiTS procedure aims to detect changepoints in 
    a piecewise linear signal where their number and location are unknown. 
    Changes can occur in the intercept, slope or both of linear trends.
    Optical compositing and on-the-fly spectral-index calculation support
    Sentinel-2 and Landsat Analysis Ready Data supplied by a FORCE data cube.
